Playwright test conventions for `tests/`. The constitution (always-on) states non-negotiables; this file adds standards, examples, and review smells.

**Section summaries:** (1) Prefer role-based locators in priority order; CSS/XPath needs review. (2) Wait with web-first `expect()` only. (3) POMs hold locators and actions, not assertions. (4) One login per auth family via setup + `storageState`. (5) Unique, self-cleaning data; no shared-order coupling. (6) Exactly one tag per `test()`; `@destructive` only for global shared state. (7) Factories, static invalid sets, env secrets, route enums. (8) API fixture for setup/teardown and contracts. (9) Mock dependencies and failure modes, not the SUT endpoint. (10) axe + keyboard checks on key surfaces. (11) Web-first assertions; soft and screenshot rules. (12) Isolation, clocks, locale — not retries/workers as band-aids.

## 1. Locators

Prefer locators that mirror how users and assistive tech find controls: `getByRole` → `getByLabel` / `getByPlaceholder` → `getByText` → `getByTestId` only when nothing user-facing fits. Raw CSS or XPath is a smell and should be flagged in review.

✅ **Good**

```typescript
await page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Save draft' }).click();
await page.getByLabel('Session title').fill(uniqueTitle);
```

❌ **Bad**

```typescript
await page.locator('#app > div.panel:nth-child(2) button.primary').click();
await page.locator('//button[contains(@class,"submit")]').click();
```

**Why:** Role and name locators track accessibility and survive markup refactors; CSS/XPath ties tests to implementation detail.

## 2. Waiting

Use Playwright’s web-first assertions and auto-waiting; never sleep or snapshot visibility into a boolean before asserting.

✅ **Good**

```typescript
await expect(page.getByRole('heading', { name: 'Dashboard' })).toBeVisible();
await expect(page.getByText('Item saved')).toHaveCount(1);
```

❌ **Bad**

```typescript
await page.waitForTimeout(3000);
expect(await page.getByRole('alert').isVisible()).toBe(true);
```

**Why:** Fixed delays flake under load; `isVisible()` bypasses retry semantics and races the UI.

## 3. Page Object Model

One POM per page or reusable component; define locators in the constructor; methods express user actions and return navigations or components; keep assertions in specs, not in POMs.

✅ **Good**

```typescript
export class SchedulePanel {
  constructor(private readonly root: Locator) {}

  async openNewSlot(): Promise<SlotEditor> {
    await this.root.getByRole('button', { name: 'Add slot' }).click();
    return new SlotEditor(this.root.page());
  }
}
```

❌ **Bad**

```typescript
export class SchedulePanel {
  async openNewSlot() {
    await this.root.locator('.add-btn').click();
    await expect(this.root.getByText('Editor')).toBeVisible(); // assertion in POM
  }
}
```

**Why:** POMs stay reusable action layers; specs own expectations and failure messages.

## 4. Auth

Log in once per credential family in the setup project and persist `storageState` (`AUTH_FILE` for the main family, `ALT_AUTH_FILE` for the second). Two-family flows open a second context with `browser.newContext({ storageState: ALT_AUTH_FILE })`. Read credentials from `process.env`; never log in inside each test.

✅ **Good**

```typescript
// playwright.config.ts — setup project writes AUTH_FILE / ALT_AUTH_FILE
test.use({ storageState: AUTH_FILE });

test('two viewers see the same booking', async ({ browser }) => {
  const alt = await browser.newContext({ storageState: ALT_AUTH_FILE });
  const altPage = await alt.newPage();
  // ...
  await alt.close();
});
```

❌ **Bad**

```typescript
test.beforeEach(async ({ page }) => {
  await page.goto('/login');
  await page.getByLabel('Email').fill('demo@example.com');
  await page.getByLabel('Password').fill('secret123');
  await page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Sign in' }).click();
});
```

**Why:** Setup auth is fast and stable; per-test UI login adds minutes and flakes on session limits.

## 5. Test data

Every test creates unique data (factory or `Date.now()` suffix) and cleans up in `afterEach` / API teardown. Tests must not depend on run order or on another student’s rows in the shared environment.

✅ **Good**

```typescript
test('creates a one-off workshop @smoke', async ({ request, page }) => {
  const title = buildWorkshopTitle(); // factory + Date.now()
  const id = await createWorkshopViaApi(request, { title });
  try {
    await page.goto(`/workshops/${id}`);
    await expect(page.getByRole('heading', { name: title })).toBeVisible();
  } finally {
    await deleteWorkshopViaApi(request, id);
  }
});
```

❌ **Bad**

```typescript
test('uses the shared workshop', async ({ page }) => {
  await page.goto('/workshops/shared-demo-workshop'); // assumes prior test or manual seed
});
```

**Why:** Parallel workers and shared tenants make shared fixtures nondeterministic and hostile to CI.

## 6. Tags

Put exactly one tag on each `test()`: `@smoke` (every PR), `@sanity` (every push), `@regression` (full suite on manual dispatch), `@api`, `@e2e`, or `@destructive` (only when mutating shared/global state both families rely on — run with `--workers=1`). Never tag `describe()`. Own-data tests are not `@destructive`. Any state-mutating test reverts in `afterEach` / `afterAll`.

✅ **Good**

```typescript
test('lists open sessions @sanity', async ({ page }) => { /* ... */ });

test('disables org-wide notifications @destructive', async ({ page }) => {
  /* mutates tenant setting both auth families share; revert in afterAll */
});
```

❌ **Bad**

```typescript
test.describe('@smoke', () => {
  test('dashboard loads', async ({ page }) => { /* tag on describe */ });
});

test('deletes my draft note @destructive', async ({ page }) => {
  /* only touches data this test created — wrong tag */
});
```

**Why:** Tags drive CI selectors; mis-tagged suites either skip coverage or serialize the whole run unnecessarily.

## 7. Test data sources

Build dynamic values in `test-data/factories/` (Faker + `Date.now()`), keep static invalid sets in `test-data/*.ts`, read secrets and base URLs from `process.env`, and centralize repeated strings in enums (e.g. `test-data/routes.ts`).

✅ **Good**

```typescript
import { Routes } from '../test-data/routes';
import { buildInviteEmail } from '../test-data/factories/invite.factory';

await page.goto(`${process.env.BASE_URL}${Routes.TeamSettings}`);
const email = buildInviteEmail();
```

❌ **Bad**

```typescript
await page.goto('https://staging.example.com/team/settings');
const email = 'testuser@gmail.com';
```

**Why:** One source of truth prevents drift between specs, envs, and invalid-input catalogs.

## 8. API

Use the `request` fixture for prerequisite data, teardown, and contract checks; do not walk the UI to seed state when an API exists.

✅ **Good**

```typescript
test('shows assigned mentor @api', async ({ request, page }) => {
  const pairingId = await assignMentorViaApi(request, { mentorId, menteeId });
  await page.goto(`/pairings/${pairingId}`);
  await expect(page.getByText(mentorDisplayName)).toBeVisible();
});
```

❌ **Bad**

```typescript
test('shows assigned mentor', async ({ page }) => {
  await page.goto('/admin/pairings');
  // 15 UI steps to create pairing before the assertion under test
});
```

**Why:** API setup is faster, clearer, and failures point at the endpoint instead of incidental UI.

## 9. Network mocking

Use `page.route` / `route.fulfill` to simulate 500, 503, timeout, empty lists, or malformed payloads on dependency endpoints; never mock the endpoint whose behavior the test is meant to prove.

✅ **Good**

```typescript
await page.route('**/api/notifications/unread', (route) =>
  route.fulfill({ status: 503, body: 'Service Unavailable' }),
);
await page.goto('/inbox');
await expect(page.getByRole('alert')).toHaveText(/notifications unavailable/i);
```

❌ **Bad**

```typescript
await page.route('**/api/bookings', (route) =>
  route.fulfill({ status: 200, body: JSON.stringify({ items: [] }) }),
);
test('creates booking via POST /api/bookings @api', async ({ request }) => {
  // mocking the SUT while claiming to test it
});
```

**Why:** Mocking the system under test makes the assertion vacuous; mock the edges to exercise error UI and resilience.

## 10. Accessibility

Run `@axe-core/playwright` with `wcag2a` / `wcag2aa` on key pages, plus at least one keyboard-operability path per critical flow. Working `getByRole(..., { name })` locators are evidence of exposed accessible names.

✅ **Good**

```typescript
import AxeBuilder from '@axe-core/playwright';

test('settings page a11y @regression', async ({ page }) => {
  await page.goto('/settings/profile');
  const results = await new AxeBuilder({ page })
    .withTags(['wcag2a', 'wcag2aa'])
    .analyze();
  expect(results.violations).toEqual([]);

  await page.keyboard.press('Tab');
  await expect(page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Update profile' })).toBeFocused();
});
```

❌ **Bad**

```typescript
test('settings page', async ({ page }) => {
  await page.goto('/settings/profile');
  // no scan; only clicks with mouse-specific locators
  await page.locator('.update').click();
});
```

**Why:** Automated rules catch systemic issues; keyboard checks prove focus order matches real use.

## 11. Assertions

Default to web-first `expect(locator)` matchers; use `expect.soft` when one failure should not abort a multi-check diagnostic; use `toHaveScreenshot` only with dynamic regions masked.

✅ **Good**

```typescript
await expect(page.getByRole('status')).toHaveText('Saved');
await expect.soft(page.getByRole('listitem')).toHaveCount(3);
await expect(page).toHaveScreenshot('calendar.png', {
  mask: [page.getByRole('timer')],
});
```

❌ **Bad**

```typescript
const text = await page.locator('.status').textContent();
expect(text).toBe('Saved');
await expect(page).toHaveScreenshot('calendar.png'); // clock and avatars unmasked
```

**Why:** Locator assertions retry; string snapshots and bare screenshots false-fail on time and content noise.

## 12. Anti-flake

Enable retries in CI only, keep full test isolation, use `page.clock` when availability or calendars depend on “now”, pin locale and timezone in config, and retain trace on first retry. Treat `retries: 5` or `workers: 1` as hiding problems, not fixes.

✅ **Good**

```typescript
test('slot opens at start time @e2e', async ({ page }) => {
  await page.clock.install({ time: new Date('2026-06-01T09:00:00Z') });
  await page.goto('/availability');
  await expect(page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Book 9:00 AM' })).toBeEnabled();
});
```

❌ **Bad**

```typescript
// playwright.config.ts
retries: 5, // “make it green”
workers: 1, // serialize entire suite instead of fixing shared state
test('booking', async ({ page }) => {
  await page.waitForTimeout(2000); // hope the slot list loaded
});
```

**Why:** Deterministic time and isolation remove race classes; retries without root-cause analysis waste CI time.

## Refusals (hard stops)

Never weaken or delete an assertion to pass a test — that is a conflict to raise, not a negotiation.

Never heal a real product bug in the test layer; triage, file a defect, or fix the app.

Never replace a role-based locator with CSS or XPath to “unblock” a run.

Never use `waitForTimeout`, a hardcoded credential, or the `any` type in fixtures or POMs.

Each refusal is a stop-the-line conflict for the agent; preferences and shortcuts do not override them.
